WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.838
This lesson covers replication: keeping copies of data on several machines so a failure does not lose anything.

00:00:08.188 --> 00:00:12.925
Imagine one student takes the class notes, and two friends keep photocopies.

00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:16.475
If the note taker is sick, a friend can share the notes.

00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:20.849
But if the friends copy only once a week, their copies may be out of date.

00:00:21.299 --> 00:00:28.288
The big question in replication is: when do we tell the teacher the notes are saved? After one copy, or after all of them?

00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:31.717
Here is leader based replication.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.256
A client sends a write to the leader.

00:00:34.706 --> 00:00:37.853
The leader appends it to its log and sends it to the followers.

00:00:38.303 --> 00:00:42.378
Once enough followers confirm, the leader replies OK to the client.

00:00:43.728 --> 00:00:44.726
Vocabulary.

00:00:45.176 --> 00:00:49.994
Synchronous replication waits for followers before replying: safe, but slower.

00:00:50.444 --> 00:00:56.145
Asynchronous replication replies immediately: fast, but a crash can lose the latest writes.

00:00:56.595 --> 00:01:02.748
Replication lag is how far behind a follower is. Reading from a lagging follower can show you old data.

00:01:03.198 --> 00:01:06.693
Failover is promoting a follower to leader when the leader dies.

00:01:08.043 --> 00:01:14.231
Consensus is how replicas agree on one leader and one log order, even when machines crash.

00:01:14.681 --> 00:01:19.824
Raft does this with terms, which are numbered election rounds, and a majority vote.

00:01:20.274 --> 00:01:27.507
A write is committed once a majority of nodes has it in their log. With five nodes, two can fail and the system still works.

00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:33.878
Split brain, two leaders at once, is prevented because two majorities always overlap.

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:44.725
Copies protect data; acks decide safety versus speed; majorities prevent split brain. See the object store and Kafka log designs.
